"Rabbits are superhumans whose worth lies in being summoned."
"Summoned Rabbits grow rich and are yanked away by summons at awkward moments."
"Most Rabbits are never summoned and have no skills and no money."
"Rabbit is the errand-runner class, about 1.1% of selections by the current year's numbers."
"Its Artonan name is Ryeh-b't, after a small flying reptile of Artona 3."
"The name also fits how Rabbits vanish in a flash when summoned."
"Artonans made the class because they wanted magical housekeepers and secretaries."
"Rabbits exist on every Artonan resource world."
"Human Rabbits mostly work as assistants and errand-runners on the Triplanets."
"Rabbits are summoned more often than any other class."
"With normal luck a Rabbit is summoned a couple of times a month, for a few hours each."
"The average Rabbit earns about half a million dollars a year for almost no work."
"The Rabbit skill list is huge and barely explored; about thirty starters are sure bets."
"Pre-affixation descriptions of Rabbit skills are vague."
"Rabbits are soft-restricted from talents as quest rewards and must level for new ones."
"A Rabbit sits on the Anesidoran High Council."
"Some Avowed wait their whole grace period hoping Rabbit appears on the market."
"A 25-year-old S-rank Rabbit's only skill, floor polishing, made an iceless skating rink."
"A 17-year-old A-rank Rabbit famously declared herself retired before finishing high school."
"A B-rank typed up the entire Rabbit skill list online."
"All Rabbits receive at least one skill; Rabbit is the only class with a starting Appeal bonus."
"A Rabbit's summons response timer is 48 seconds; other classes get hours or days of notice."
"Summoners may not pay Rabbits in skills or spell impressions for regular services."
"For extraordinary service Rabbits may still get foundation points, tools or spell teaching."
"Rabbits get new skills only by leveling starters or long-term assignment to approved parties."
"A Rabbit's quest rejection-to-acceptance ratio may not exceed 1 in 20."
"B-rank Rabbits pick one spell impression before affixation and one after."
"A B-rank Rabbit may pick one B-rank skill, or a D-rank and an F-rank."
"Rabbits get the Wardrobe and may choose their primary class trait."
"Even well-geared Rabbits are usually summoned only a few times a month."
"Rabbit signing gifts: 3 foundation points, The Fragile Atmosphere or Wardrobe Change."
"The System gives Rabbits proportionally very little foundational enhancement."
"Rabbit is a choice, easy to trade out of; picking it signals priorities apart from hero work."
"Wizards want friendly domestic help more than warriors, yet Rabbit numbers are kept low."
"Many Rabbits take extra Appeal and allow System personality changes; they are social and cute."
"Many Rabbits are business-minded, planning skills as careers on Earth or for Artonans."
"Rabbits are known for liking money, and gambling is popular among them."
"The worst of Rabbit life is being kept busy by Artonans; the best is eternal youth."
"Some rich Rabbits are lazy, envied and hated by others."
"Rabbits start with one or two skills, weak spells and few foundation points."
"The Rabbit skill list is full of cleaning and kitchen skills."
"In class satisfaction surveys, Rabbit ranks just below Healer."
"Rabbits are advised to stay well-dressed and packed to leave on under a minute's notice."
"The class grants nearly instant summons for non-emergencies, with limited refusals."
"More summonable humans are popular with most wizards and moderately popular among Anesidorans."
"Proposals to raise Rabbit assignments have been held back for years by certain others' adamance."
"Wizard factions split: some want Avowed useful to them personally, others push against it."
"A Rabbit business association's Diamond Star Employer program links new Rabbits to part-time jobs."
